### Bug Description

Connecting Hermes Desktop over SSH to a Windows remote running Windows OpenSSH Server (`sshd`, default `DefaultShell = cmd.exe`) fails intermittently (~80–85% failure rate) with 20-second SSH command timeouts:

- `Error: SSH operation to <remote-user>@<windows-ssh-host> timed out. The connection may be half-open (e.g. after sleep); reconnecting.`
- Or, when `probeWindowsRemote()` happens to succeed on an attempt and the immediately following `assertWindowsRemoteInstallUpdateClear()` hangs:
  `Error: Could not prove that the remote Hermes install is clear for SSH startup.`

Additionally, every timed-out probe leaves a zombie `powershell.exe` process (~67 MB RAM each) permanently hung in the remote Windows `Services` session waiting on `[Console]::In.ReadToEnd()`.

Even on the ~15% of attempts where the connection eventually succeeds, `refreshSshConnectionProfiles()` in `apps/desktop/electron/main.ts` unconditionally runs POSIX `listRemoteHermesProfiles()` / `readRemoteInstallId()` against the Windows remote, logging `Unsafe remote Hermes home.` / `Could not list remote Hermes profiles.` on every refresh cycle.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Set up a Windows 11 remote host with the built-in Windows OpenSSH Server (`sshd`, standard `DefaultShell = C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe`) and a valid SSH key.
2. In Hermes Desktop, add an SSH connection to `<remote-user>@<windows-ssh-host>` and click Connect / Reconnect.
3. Alternatively, run the standalone minimal reproducer below from any client against the Windows OpenSSH target:

